I'm going to go with #3041 for the code, but @nez0b made an important observation about the case when representations contain both positive and negative signs. For that reason I'd like to credit both of you for the solution.

@cosenal both hackers made important contributions to what is the final product being merged here. Can we give both hackers the bounty? Happy to provide the extra cash if that's the issue.

@zkasuran please comment here so you can be assigned.

Nice, this is very close to the implementation path I was looking at as well. Since you already have the Z-error aggregation and OperationRepresentation mapping underway, I’ll avoid duplicating that work.

I can instead look into the validation/benchmarking side: comparing the aggregated path against the current PEC implementation under fixed seeds, measuring the reduction in QPD norm / required samples, and checking estimator equivalence on small circuits. I’d also be interested in exploring how the abstraction could generalize beyond the Z-only case once the initial API settles.

@anishbhujbal7, if there are particular benchmark cases or tests that would be useful for your prototype, feel free to ping me. @MathysRennela, happy to take another slice instead if there’s something you think would be more useful.

@natestemen  Hi! I opened a PR for this: #3053 
The notebook walks through preparing |1_L 0_L> with the [[4,2,2]] encoding circuit (4-qubit GHZ + a logical X), measuring the XXXX/ZZZZ syndromes with two ancillas, then comparing four cases under 2% local depolarizing noise: ideal, noisy unmitigated, mitiq.rem.post_select only, and post-selection combined with mitiq.zne.execute_with_zne (Richardson extrapolation). The discussion section covers the shot-discarding overhead from post-selection, as suggested in the issue.
I noticed there's already an open PR (#3025) that also references this issue as part of a larger multi-issue PR — happy to adjust scope or defer if that one's the preferred direction, just wanted to put this together since the deliverable was pretty clearly scoped. looking forward to your review
